---
title: The model facade
summary: skoro-model. ModelServer and ModelClient, typed Q handles as ownership tokens, mark-and-sweep collection, and the two client tiers.
order: 7
---

`skoro-model` turns the untyped graph into the API a user holds.

## Server and client

`ModelServer<B>` owns the `Graph`. `ModelClient<B>` is a cheap, cloneable handle to it, and the
only routes from a client to the graph itself are `server_call` and `server_call_mut`. Everything
else goes through two traits:

- `ModelReaderOps`: evaluate, differentiate, generate, read terms and bindings, and add
  constants. Nothing on it changes the density, the proposal, the free and observed set, or the
  batch sizes the model accepts.
- `ModelBuilderOps: ModelReaderOps`: everything that does. Add density terms (quantities,
  proposals, factors) and parameters, `observe`, `demote_to_parameter`, `stacked_tangent`,
  `remove` and `remove_closure`.

> [!DECISION]
> See [Client tiers](../../design/client-tiers/).

## Typed handles

`Q<B, T>` wraps an `Id` with a `PhantomData` tag `T`, the quantity type. The builder API is typed
(you can't pass a rotation where a vector is expected). The arena underneath is erased.

## Ownership and collection

A `Q` is an **ownership token**; a bare `Id` is a weak reference. `cleanup` is a mark-and-sweep
garbage collector:

- **Mark** walks out from the *roots*, the ids named by live handles plus every term in the
  model's likelihood arena, and marks what it reaches. Marked means keep.
- **Sweep** removes every unmarked node, children before parents, through the same leaf-only
  `remove` a caller would use.

Marking follows *references*, from a node to what it depends on: its parents, the quantity a
density scores (even one it does not read), and every node it binds. It never walks downward, so
holding `x` does not keep a child `y = f(x)` alive; only a handle on `y`, or a term that reads it,
does. Because every term is a root, a sweep can never change $\log p$ or $\log q$. Three
properties follow:

- Nothing has to *report* orphaning; reachability is recomputed.
- Every node kind is collectable, including constants and parameters nothing reaches.
- The sweep cannot fail.

It runs at the mutations that *reduce* reachability (`remove`, `remove_closure`,
`demote_to_parameter`) and not on the way in, because sweeping on every `add_*` would make building
an $n$-node model cost $O(n^2)$. Dropping a `Q` never collects by itself: it only marks the
registry dirty, and the next `cleanup` does the work.

> [!WARNING] A collected parameter leaves the free set
> A `Parameter` that no handle names and no term reads is garbage like anything else. That
> changes neither density, but it takes the parameter out of the model's free set, and a bare
> `Id` or a `TangentLayout` built over it earlier then fails with `NodeNotFound`. Keep a `Q` for
> anything you mean to optimize.

> [!DECISION]
> See [Handles own nodes](../../design/ownership/).

## Distributions, factors, and readers

`Distribution` and `Factor` (see [Distributions and factors](../../guide/distributions-and-factors/))
are defined here, as are the readers that consume bindings: `least_squares::residual_layout`,
`information`/`information_root`/`information_blocks`, `jacobian_vjp`/`jacobian_jvp`, and
`coverage::{terms_missing, require_role}`.

## Batch pinning

Two calls pin a model to one batch size: `add_batched_constant` (one value per row) and
`stacked_tangent` (batch-pinned linearization anchors). Everything else broadcasts.

